-ruff
+# Direct import in `tests/catalogue_oracle.py` (`yaml.safe_dump`). It resolves
+# today only through `fastapi[standard]` -> `uvicorn[standard]`, so declare it
+# here: the oracle must not rely on a transitive that an upstream extras change
+# could drop. Left unpinned on purpose — the oracle asserts only
+# predicted-infeasible => INFEASIBLE, which is insensitive to which equivalent
+# YAML `safe_dump` emits.
+pyyaml
+# For CI. Exact pins keep the fresh-venv install and CI toolchain
+# reproducible. `fakeredis` is fenced below 3 instead of pinned exactly:
+# the store tests run the production `stores/redis.py` WATCH/MULTI path on
+# fakeredis, so a major bump must be a deliberate change, while 2.x fixes
+# keep flowing. The currently resolving version is 2.38.0.
+pytest==9.1.1
+pytest-cov==7.1.0
+fakeredis>=2,<3
+# `starlette.testclient` (re-exported as `fastapi.testclient`) is built on
+# `httpx2`; when `httpx2` is missing it falls back to `httpx` and emits
+# `StarletteDeprecationWarning: Using \`httpx\` with \`starlette.testclient\` is
+# deprecated; install \`httpx2\` instead.` Neither `fastapi[standard]` nor
+# Starlette's base install pulls `httpx2` — it lives only in Starlette's `full`
+# extra — so declare the supported test-client backend explicitly. Test-only:
+# production imports `httpx` (the diagnostic runner), never `starlette.testclient`.
+# Fenced below 3 like `fakeredis`: a major bump must be deliberate.
+httpx2>=2,<3
+# Ruff is intentionally pinned: Ruff's *default* rule set expands between
+# releases (e.g. 0.15.x -> 0.16.x widened it materially). The explicit
+# `tool.ruff.lint.select` in pyproject.toml keeps the contract honest even if
+# this pin is later bumped.
+ruff==0.15.22
